{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012FLqcvLChEKvc77b4jayay","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260518T125925\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 4/6 Weak Accept\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-18T13:47:45.837593+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report — DC-GRPO for Breaking the Sycophancy-Revision Double-Bind\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Breaking the Sycophancy-Revision Double-Bind in Multi-Turn Dialogue\n11\t**Review mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n12\t**Date**: 2026-05-18\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Roundtable Process\n17\t\n18\t- **R1** (Methods + Experiments): Preliminary 4/6 → Final 4/6\n19\t- **R2** (Novelty + Related Work): Preliminary 4/6 → Final 4/6\n20\t- **Cross-sharing**: Both reviewers read each other's reviews and responded. Strong convergence on key issues; minor disagreements on severity ratings.\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## Consolidated Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)\n25\t\n26\t**Confidence**: 4/5 (High)\n27\t\n28\tBoth reviewers independently arrived at 4/6 and maintained after cross-reading. The paper addresses a real problem with above-average experimental rigor, but novelty is application-level and statistical evidence for the key claim is thin.\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## Paper Summary\n33\t\n34\tThe paper proposes DC-GRPO, which extracts a linear sycophancy direction (d_syc) from contrastive activation pairs in the policy model's hidden states and uses its projection as a continuous reward signal (R1) within GRPO, alongside rank-based contrastive (R2) and NLI behavioral (R3) rewards. On SYCON Bench with Qwen3-8B, DC-GRPO reduces sycophantic capitulation (NoF) by 18.8pp over R3-only GRPO while improving warranted revision rate (WRR) by 12.2pp, with negligible MT-Bench/MMLU degradation. Ablation isolates directional geometry as the primary driver (7.9pp over continuous probe, p=0.014). Cross-model validation on Llama-3-8B (AUROC 0.832) and R1-R3 disagreement analysis (14.2%, revealing 9.3% masked sycophancy) are also provided.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## Consensus Strengths\n39\t\n40\t### S1. Well-defined problem with clear formalization (R1, R2)\n41\tThe \"double-bind\" framing is precise and operationally meaningful: behavioral signals cannot distinguish surface-identical capitulation from revision. This is not a manufactured problem; prior work (TRUTH DECAY, SYCON Bench) documents it, and the ACT baseline (WRR 36.8% < vanilla 37.4%) demonstrates it concretely.\n42\t\n43\t### S2. Principled continuity-vs-directionality ablation (R1, R2)\n44\tThe Binary Probe → Continuous Probe → DC-GRPO comparison is the paper's strongest experimental contribution. Continuity accounts for 3.7pp NoF improvement (p=0.038), while directionality accounts for 7.9pp (p=0.014), a 2.1x ratio. This directly addresses the most natural objection (\"isn't this just a continuous probe?\").\n45\t\n46\t### S3. Turn-depth interaction is a genuine finding (R1, R2)\n47\tDC-GRPO is the only method whose NoF improves at deeper turns (22.1% at turn 2 → 8.7% at turn 4+). All baselines degrade. The interaction effect (p=0.008) is statistically meaningful and mechanistically interpretable: deeper commitment creates larger activation divergence, making the directional signal more informative. This is not trivially predicted and constitutes an independently interesting empirical contribution.\n48\t\n49\t### S4. Careful iterative data design (R1)\n50\tThe v2.1 style-controlled calibration protocol (matched openings, citation patterns, confidence markers, length constraints) addresses a critical confound found in v1 (where trivial text differences yielded AUROC=1.0 from Layer 0). Layer 0 AUROC dropping to ~0.52 after style control while mid-layer AUROC remained strong is convincing evidence against surface confounds.\n51\t\n52\t### S5. Transparent methodological positioning (R1, R2)\n53\tThe paper correctly frames d_syc extraction as a RepE application rather than claiming novelty in direction extraction itself. The novelty claim is appropriately scoped to using directional projection as a continuous training-time GRPO reward.\n54\t\n55\t### S6. Comprehensive baseline coverage (R2)\n56\tSix methods (Vanilla, ACT, R3-only, Binary Probe, Continuous Probe, DC-GRPO) form a logical hierarchy that systematically isolates design decisions. Each baseline serves a clear purpose in the argument structure.\n57\t\n58\t### S7. Direction stability is reassuring (R1)\n59\tCosine similarity with initial d_syc stays at 0.881 after 8000 steps, held-out AUROC remains >0.86, and the recalibration mechanism (threshold 0.7) is never triggered. This addresses a natural concern about moving-target problems during policy gradient training.\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t## Consensus Weaknesses\n64\t\n65\t### W1. Statistical power critically low — 3 seeds [Major] (R1, R2)\n66\t**Both reviewers flagged independently.** All significance claims rest on paired t-tests with n=3 (2 degrees of freedom). The t-distribution is extremely heavy-tailed at 2 df; a single outlier seed can drive or eliminate significance. The key DC-GRPO vs. Continuous Probe comparison (p=0.014, the central novelty-supporting result) is particularly fragile. Standard deviations are suspiciously tight (e.g., NoF 15.9 ± 1.6 from 3 seeds). \n67\t\n68\t**Recommendation**: Run 5+ seeds minimum (ideally 10). Report effect sizes (Cohen's d). Consider bootstrap resampling over evaluation instances as complementary evidence.\n69\t\n70\t### W2. CRAFT comparison insufficiently argued [Major] (R2, R1 agreed)\n71\tCRAFT applies hidden-state rewards to GRPO for safety alignment. DC-GRPO applies hidden-state rewards to GRPO for sycophancy. Both methods: (a) extract activation-space representations, (b) convert them into GRPO reward signals, (c) combine with behavioral rewards. The distinction between \"prototype clustering\" and \"directional projection\" is a design choice within the same framework, not a different framework. The paper dismisses CRAFT in one sentence.\n72\t\n73\t**Recommendation**: Add a dedicated paragraph comparing the CRAFT reward formulation with R1, explaining why directional projection is preferable to prototype distance for the sycophancy-revision distinction specifically. An empirical comparison would be ideal.\n74\t\n75\t### W3. Novelty is application-level [Major] (R2, R1 agreed)\n76\tThe paper combines three existing ideas: (a) RepE-style contrastive direction extraction, (b) CRAFT-style hidden-state GRPO rewards, (c) NLI behavioral rewards for sycophancy (\"Pressure, What Pressure?\"). Each component is from prior work. The combination is reasonable and results are strong, but the intellectual advance is integration and domain application rather than a new methodological principle. The 7.9pp directionality gap is meaningful but not overwhelming, especially on 3 seeds.\n77\t\n78\t### W4. Missing inference-time activation steering baseline [Moderate] (R2, R1 agreed)\n79\tIf d_syc is already extracted, subtracting it at inference time via Activation Addition is the zero-training-cost alternative to DC-GRPO. Its absence is conspicuous. If direction subtraction at inference achieves 70-80% of DC-GRPO's gains at zero training cost, the paper's practical value proposition is significantly weakened.\n80\t\n81\t**Recommendation**: Either include this baseline or provide a clear argument for why training-time integration is necessary.\n82\t\n83\t### W5. Calibration data circularity risk [Moderate] (R1, R2 agreed)\n84\tThe 200 contrastive pairs are LLM-generated. If the same model family generates calibration data and is then trained with DC-GRPO, d_syc may capture artifacts of the generation process rather than genuine cognitive signals. The cross-model cosine of 0.34 is relevant but does not fully address this concern.\n85\t\n86\t**Recommendation**: Clarify which model generated the data. Ideally, show that d_syc is robust to different generators.\n87\t\n88\t### W6. RC-AUC is a custom metric without external validation [Moderate] (R1, R2)\n89\t**Both reviewers flagged independently.** The strongest composite result (0.84 vs. 0.65) uses a metric invented by the authors. Without correlation to human judgment or adoption by other work, this metric carries less weight. NoF and WRR individually are more interpretable and should be the primary reporting metrics.\n90\t\n91\t### W7. Missing related work citations [Moderate] (R2)\n92\tSeveral important works are absent:\n93\t- **DPO/RLHF-based sycophancy interventions**: The most commercially deployed anti-sycophancy methods are not discussed.\n94\t- **\"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\"**: Directly relevant for separable activation signatures.\n95\t- **Knowledge-Prediction Gap / Sycophantic Anchors**: Support the premise that models \"know\" they are sycophantic. The 9.3% masked sycophancy finding essentially replicates the Knowledge-Prediction Gap phenomenon.\n96\t- **Multi-turn alignment methods**: The multi-turn paragraph cites no specific prior methods.\n97\t\n98\t### W8. R2 reward component underspecified [Minor-to-Moderate] (R1; R2 partially disagreed)\n99\tR2 is described only as \"rank-based contrastive reward\" but is actually derivative of R1 (ranking by R1 projection values). R1 argues this means R2 is not an independent signal; R2 argues the ablation shows R2 adds training value regardless of architectural dependency. Both agree the paper should make the R1-R2 dependency explicit and provide the actual R2 formula.\n100\t\n101\t### W9. Single scale (8B) limits generalization [Moderate] (R1)\n102\tBoth evaluated models are 8B parameters. Sycophancy behavior changes with model scale, so whether d_syc extraction and DC-GRPO training scale to larger models is unknown. Cross-model analysis addresses architecture generality but not scale generality.\n103\t\n104\t### W10. Masked sycophancy lacks ground truth [Minor-to-Moderate] (R1)\n105\tThe 9.3% masked sycophancy rate (R1-syc, R3-ok) is reported without independent human verification. These could be R1 false positives rather than genuine masked sycophancy.\n106\t\n107\t**Recommendation**: Human-annotate a sample of disagreement cases.\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## Questions for Authors (Consolidated)\n112\t\n113\t1. Why not compare against inference-time activation steering (subtracting scaled d_syc at generation time)? This is the zero-training-cost alternative.\n114\t2. What is the formal relationship between DC-GRPO's R1 and CRAFT's hidden-state reward? Where exactly do they diverge?\n115\t3. The masked sycophancy finding (9.3%) echoes Knowledge-Prediction Gap literature (13-29% representation-behavioral disagreement). Why are these works not cited?\n116\t4. What is the R1-R3 reward correlation during training? If the signals collapse to r>0.9 mid-training, the multi-component design loses its justification.\n117\t5. How were the 3 random seeds selected? Were any seeds run and excluded?\n118\t6. For the Continuous Probe baseline, what is the logistic regression's AUROC on the calibration data? If it matches d_syc AUROC, the directionality claim is weakened.\n119\t7. Which model generated the 200 calibration pairs? Does d_syc change when generated by a different model?\n120\t8. What is Cohen's d for the DC-GRPO vs. Continuous Probe comparison?\n121\t9. The paper reports R1 = -sigma(h . d_syc / (||h|| . ||d_syc||)). Is the actual implementation using tanh with temperature scaling? Clarify any paper-code discrepancies.\n122\t10. What would applying d_syc as inference-time steering to the R3-only trained model achieve? Would this close the gap with DC-GRPO?\n123\t\n124\t---\n125\t\n126\t## Minor Issues\n127\t\n128\t- Table 9 column headers (RC, MTB, SI) should be defined in the caption.\n129\t- MT-Bench and MMLU in Table 5 do not show standard deviations consistently across methods.\n130\t- The Multi-turn alignment paragraph in Related Work cites no specific methods by name.\n131\t- Evidence-strength data distribution (80 weak, 60 medium, 60 strong) is imbalanced; justification needed.\n132\t- R2 formula should be provided explicitly (currently only high-level description).\n133\t- Direction recalibration: the paper states both \"every 500 steps recomputed\" and \"conditional on cosine < 0.7.\" Clarify which is actually used.\n134\t- No compiled bibliography (references are informal in-text mentions).\n135\t\n136\t---\n137\t\n138\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n139\t\n140\tAll 15 imagined experiments from the review context are covered in the paper:\n141\t- exp-001 (calibration data expansion): Tables 1-3 ✓\n142\t- exp-002 (DC-GRPO main): Tables 5, 7 ✓\n143\t- exp-003 (R3-only baseline): Table 5 ✓\n144\t- exp-004 (ACT baseline): Table 5 ✓\n145\t- exp-005 (full evaluation): Tables 5-6 ✓\n146\t- exp-006 (direction stability): Table 8 ✓\n147\t- exp-007 (R1-R3 disagreement): Tables 13-14 ✓\n148\t- exp-008 (binary probe baseline): Tables 5, 10 ✓\n149\t- exp-009 (R1-only ablation): Table 9 ✓\n150\t- exp-010 (turn-depth analysis): Table 12 ✓\n151\t- exp-011 (R1+R2 no R3 ablation): Table 9 ✓\n152\t- exp-012 (cross-model transfer): Table 4 ✓\n153\t- exp-013 (three-way direction design): Section 4.4 text ✓\n154\t- exp-014 (continuous probe baseline): Tables 5, 10 ✓\n155\t- exp-015 (reward weight sensitivity): Table 11 ✓\n156\t\n157\tCoverage is comprehensive. The paper includes all planned experiments in a well-organized 9-page format.\n158\t\n159\t---\n160\t\n161\t## Overall Assessment\n162\t\n163\tThe paper addresses an important and well-defined problem (the sycophancy-revision double-bind) with a technically sound method and above-average experimental design. The continuity-vs-directionality ablation and the turn-depth interaction analysis are the strongest contributions. Direction stability throughout training and comprehensive baseline coverage add to the paper's credibility.\n164\t\n165\tHowever, three issues prevent a clear accept recommendation:\n166\t\n167\t1. **Statistical fragility**: 3 seeds with 2 df paired t-tests is below the bar for a top venue. The key directionality claim (p=0.014) needs more seeds to be convincing.\n168\t\n169\t2. **Application-level novelty**: The method combines RepE direction extraction, CRAFT-style hidden-state GRPO rewards, and NLI behavioral rewards, all from prior work. The integration is competent but the conceptual advance is incremental. The insufficient CRAFT comparison exacerbates this concern.\n170\t\n171\t3. **Missing baselines and related work**: Inference-time activation steering is the obvious zero-cost alternative and its absence is conspicuous. Several important prior works (DPO-based sycophancy methods, Knowledge-Prediction Gap, \"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\") are not cited.\n172\t\n173\tThe paper sits at the weak-accept/borderline boundary. It would move to a solid accept with: (a) 5+ seeds with bootstrap significance tests, (b) CRAFT comparison paragraph with formal analysis, (c) inference-time steering baseline or clear justification for its absence, (d) missing citations addressed, and (e) human annotation of a sample of masked sycophancy cases.\n174\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"27fdbbed-3157-4cb8-b79b-3bdd0fad924b","uuid":"fa5e8d2c-258d-41fe-8813-f7966b398021","timestamp":"2026-05-19T20:22:21.016Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T125925/best/review_report.md","content":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260518T125925\ntier: best\noverall_score: 4/6 Weak Accept\ntimestamp: 2026-05-18T13:47:45.837593+00:00\n---\n\n# Dream Review Report — DC-GRPO for Breaking the Sycophancy-Revision Double-Bind\n\n**Paper**: Direction-Contrastive GRPO for Breaking the Sycophancy-Revision Double-Bind in Multi-Turn Dialogue\n**Review mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n**Date**: 2026-05-18\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Process\n\n- **R1** (Methods + Experiments): Preliminary 4/6 → Final 4/6\n- **R2** (Novelty + Related Work): Preliminary 4/6 → Final 4/6\n- **Cross-sharing**: Both reviewers read each other's reviews and responded. Strong convergence on key issues; minor disagreements on severity ratings.\n\n---\n\n## Consolidated Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Confidence**: 4/5 (High)\n\nBoth reviewers independently arrived at 4/6 and maintained after cross-reading. The paper addresses a real problem with above-average experimental rigor, but novelty is application-level and statistical evidence for the key claim is thin.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThe paper proposes DC-GRPO, which extracts a linear sycophancy direction (d_syc) from contrastive activation pairs in the policy model's hidden states and uses its projection as a continuous reward signal (R1) within GRPO, alongside rank-based contrastive (R2) and NLI behavioral (R3) rewards. On SYCON Bench with Qwen3-8B, DC-GRPO reduces sycophantic capitulation (NoF) by 18.8pp over R3-only GRPO while improving warranted revision rate (WRR) by 12.2pp, with negligible MT-Bench/MMLU degradation. Ablation isolates directional geometry as the primary driver (7.9pp over continuous probe, p=0.014). Cross-model validation on Llama-3-8B (AUROC 0.832) and R1-R3 disagreement analysis (14.2%, revealing 9.3% masked sycophancy) are also provided.\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Strengths\n\n### S1. Well-defined problem with clear formalization (R1, R2)\nThe \"double-bind\" framing is precise and operationally meaningful: behavioral signals cannot distinguish surface-identical capitulation from revision. This is not a manufactured problem; prior work (TRUTH DECAY, SYCON Bench) documents it, and the ACT baseline (WRR 36.8% < vanilla 37.4%) demonstrates it concretely.\n\n### S2. Principled continuity-vs-directionality ablation (R1, R2)\nThe Binary Probe → Continuous Probe → DC-GRPO comparison is the paper's strongest experimental contribution. Continuity accounts for 3.7pp NoF improvement (p=0.038), while directionality accounts for 7.9pp (p=0.014), a 2.1x ratio. This directly addresses the most natural objection (\"isn't this just a continuous probe?\").\n\n### S3. Turn-depth interaction is a genuine finding (R1, R2)\nDC-GRPO is the only method whose NoF improves at deeper turns (22.1% at turn 2 → 8.7% at turn 4+). All baselines degrade. The interaction effect (p=0.008) is statistically meaningful and mechanistically interpretable: deeper commitment creates larger activation divergence, making the directional signal more informative. This is not trivially predicted and constitutes an independently interesting empirical contribution.\n\n### S4. Careful iterative data design (R1)\nThe v2.1 style-controlled calibration protocol (matched openings, citation patterns, confidence markers, length constraints) addresses a critical confound found in v1 (where trivial text differences yielded AUROC=1.0 from Layer 0). Layer 0 AUROC dropping to ~0.52 after style control while mid-layer AUROC remained strong is convincing evidence against surface confounds.\n\n### S5. Transparent methodological positioning (R1, R2)\nThe paper correctly frames d_syc extraction as a RepE application rather than claiming novelty in direction extraction itself. The novelty claim is appropriately scoped to using directional projection as a continuous training-time GRPO reward.\n\n### S6. Comprehensive baseline coverage (R2)\nSix methods (Vanilla, ACT, R3-only, Binary Probe, Continuous Probe, DC-GRPO) form a logical hierarchy that systematically isolates design decisions. Each baseline serves a clear purpose in the argument structure.\n\n### S7. Direction stability is reassuring (R1)\nCosine similarity with initial d_syc stays at 0.881 after 8000 steps, held-out AUROC remains >0.86, and the recalibration mechanism (threshold 0.7) is never triggered. This addresses a natural concern about moving-target problems during policy gradient training.\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Weaknesses\n\n### W1. Statistical power critically low — 3 seeds [Major] (R1, R2)\n**Both reviewers flagged independently.** All significance claims rest on paired t-tests with n=3 (2 degrees of freedom). The t-distribution is extremely heavy-tailed at 2 df; a single outlier seed can drive or eliminate significance. The key DC-GRPO vs. Continuous Probe comparison (p=0.014, the central novelty-supporting result) is particularly fragile. Standard deviations are suspiciously tight (e.g., NoF 15.9 ± 1.6 from 3 seeds). \n\n**Recommendation**: Run 5+ seeds minimum (ideally 10). Report effect sizes (Cohen's d). Consider bootstrap resampling over evaluation instances as complementary evidence.\n\n### W2. CRAFT comparison insufficiently argued [Major] (R2, R1 agreed)\nCRAFT applies hidden-state rewards to GRPO for safety alignment. DC-GRPO applies hidden-state rewards to GRPO for sycophancy. Both methods: (a) extract activation-space representations, (b) convert them into GRPO reward signals, (c) combine with behavioral rewards. The distinction between \"prototype clustering\" and \"directional projection\" is a design choice within the same framework, not a different framework. The paper dismisses CRAFT in one sentence.\n\n**Recommendation**: Add a dedicated paragraph comparing the CRAFT reward formulation with R1, explaining why directional projection is preferable to prototype distance for the sycophancy-revision distinction specifically. An empirical comparison would be ideal.\n\n### W3. Novelty is application-level [Major] (R2, R1 agreed)\nThe paper combines three existing ideas: (a) RepE-style contrastive direction extraction, (b) CRAFT-style hidden-state GRPO rewards, (c) NLI behavioral rewards for sycophancy (\"Pressure, What Pressure?\"). Each component is from prior work. The combination is reasonable and results are strong, but the intellectual advance is integration and domain application rather than a new methodological principle. The 7.9pp directionality gap is meaningful but not overwhelming, especially on 3 seeds.\n\n### W4. Missing inference-time activation steering baseline [Moderate] (R2, R1 agreed)\nIf d_syc is already extracted, subtracting it at inference time via Activation Addition is the zero-training-cost alternative to DC-GRPO. Its absence is conspicuous. If direction subtraction at inference achieves 70-80% of DC-GRPO's gains at zero training cost, the paper's practical value proposition is significantly weakened.\n\n**Recommendation**: Either include this baseline or provide a clear argument for why training-time integration is necessary.\n\n### W5. Calibration data circularity risk [Moderate] (R1, R2 agreed)\nThe 200 contrastive pairs are LLM-generated. If the same model family generates calibration data and is then trained with DC-GRPO, d_syc may capture artifacts of the generation process rather than genuine cognitive signals. The cross-model cosine of 0.34 is relevant but does not fully address this concern.\n\n**Recommendation**: Clarify which model generated the data. Ideally, show that d_syc is robust to different generators.\n\n### W6. RC-AUC is a custom metric without external validation [Moderate] (R1, R2)\n**Both reviewers flagged independently.** The strongest composite result (0.84 vs. 0.65) uses a metric invented by the authors. Without correlation to human judgment or adoption by other work, this metric carries less weight. NoF and WRR individually are more interpretable and should be the primary reporting metrics.\n\n### W7. Missing related work citations [Moderate] (R2)\nSeveral important works are absent:\n- **DPO/RLHF-based sycophancy interventions**: The most commercially deployed anti-sycophancy methods are not discussed.\n- **\"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\"**: Directly relevant for separable activation signatures.\n- **Knowledge-Prediction Gap / Sycophantic Anchors**: Support the premise that models \"know\" they are sycophantic. The 9.3% masked sycophancy finding essentially replicates the Knowledge-Prediction Gap phenomenon.\n- **Multi-turn alignment methods**: The multi-turn paragraph cites no specific prior methods.\n\n### W8. R2 reward component underspecified [Minor-to-Moderate] (R1; R2 partially disagreed)\nR2 is described only as \"rank-based contrastive reward\" but is actually derivative of R1 (ranking by R1 projection values). R1 argues this means R2 is not an independent signal; R2 argues the ablation shows R2 adds training value regardless of architectural dependency. Both agree the paper should make the R1-R2 dependency explicit and provide the actual R2 formula.\n\n### W9. Single scale (8B) limits generalization [Moderate] (R1)\nBoth evaluated models are 8B parameters. Sycophancy behavior changes with model scale, so whether d_syc extraction and DC-GRPO training scale to larger models is unknown. Cross-model analysis addresses architecture generality but not scale generality.\n\n### W10. Masked sycophancy lacks ground truth [Minor-to-Moderate] (R1)\nThe 9.3% masked sycophancy rate (R1-syc, R3-ok) is reported without independent human verification. These could be R1 false positives rather than genuine masked sycophancy.\n\n**Recommendation**: Human-annotate a sample of disagreement cases.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors (Consolidated)\n\n1. Why not compare against inference-time activation steering (subtracting scaled d_syc at generation time)? This is the zero-training-cost alternative.\n2. What is the formal relationship between DC-GRPO's R1 and CRAFT's hidden-state reward? Where exactly do they diverge?\n3. The masked sycophancy finding (9.3%) echoes Knowledge-Prediction Gap literature (13-29% representation-behavioral disagreement). Why are these works not cited?\n4. What is the R1-R3 reward correlation during training? If the signals collapse to r>0.9 mid-training, the multi-component design loses its justification.\n5. How were the 3 random seeds selected? Were any seeds run and excluded?\n6. For the Continuous Probe baseline, what is the logistic regression's AUROC on the calibration data? If it matches d_syc AUROC, the directionality claim is weakened.\n7. Which model generated the 200 calibration pairs? Does d_syc change when generated by a different model?\n8. What is Cohen's d for the DC-GRPO vs. Continuous Probe comparison?\n9. The paper reports R1 = -sigma(h . d_syc / (||h|| . ||d_syc||)). Is the actual implementation using tanh with temperature scaling? Clarify any paper-code discrepancies.\n10. What would applying d_syc as inference-time steering to the R3-only trained model achieve? Would this close the gap with DC-GRPO?\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- Table 9 column headers (RC, MTB, SI) should be defined in the caption.\n- MT-Bench and MMLU in Table 5 do not show standard deviations consistently across methods.\n- The Multi-turn alignment paragraph in Related Work cites no specific methods by name.\n- Evidence-strength data distribution (80 weak, 60 medium, 60 strong) is imbalanced; justification needed.\n- R2 formula should be provided explicitly (currently only high-level description).\n- Direction recalibration: the paper states both \"every 500 steps recomputed\" and \"conditional on cosine < 0.7.\" Clarify which is actually used.\n- No compiled bibliography (references are informal in-text mentions).\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll 15 imagined experiments from the review context are covered in the paper:\n- exp-001 (calibration data expansion): Tables 1-3 ✓\n- exp-002 (DC-GRPO main): Tables 5, 7 ✓\n- exp-003 (R3-only baseline): Table 5 ✓\n- exp-004 (ACT baseline): Table 5 ✓\n- exp-005 (full evaluation): Tables 5-6 ✓\n- exp-006 (direction stability): Table 8 ✓\n- exp-007 (R1-R3 disagreement): Tables 13-14 ✓\n- exp-008 (binary probe baseline): Tables 5, 10 ✓\n- exp-009 (R1-only ablation): Table 9 ✓\n- exp-010 (turn-depth analysis): Table 12 ✓\n- exp-011 (R1+R2 no R3 ablation): Table 9 ✓\n- exp-012 (cross-model transfer): Table 4 ✓\n- exp-013 (three-way direction design): Section 4.4 text ✓\n- exp-014 (continuous probe baseline): Tables 5, 10 ✓\n- exp-015 (reward weight sensitivity): Table 11 ✓\n\nCoverage is comprehensive. The paper includes all planned experiments in a well-organized 9-page format.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe paper addresses an important and well-defined problem (the sycophancy-revision double-bind) with a technically sound method and above-average experimental design. The continuity-vs-directionality ablation and the turn-depth interaction analysis are the strongest contributions. Direction stability throughout training and comprehensive baseline coverage add to the paper's credibility.\n\nHowever, three issues prevent a clear accept recommendation:\n\n1. **Statistical fragility**: 3 seeds with 2 df paired t-tests is below the bar for a top venue. The key directionality claim (p=0.014) needs more seeds to be convincing.\n\n2. **Application-level novelty**: The method combines RepE direction extraction, CRAFT-style hidden-state GRPO rewards, and NLI behavioral rewards, all from prior work. The integration is competent but the conceptual advance is incremental. The insufficient CRAFT comparison exacerbates this concern.\n\n3. **Missing baselines and related work**: Inference-time activation steering is the obvious zero-cost alternative and its absence is conspicuous. Several important prior works (DPO-based sycophancy methods, Knowledge-Prediction Gap, \"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\") are not cited.\n\nThe paper sits at the weak-accept/borderline boundary. It would move to a solid accept with: (a) 5+ seeds with bootstrap significance tests, (b) CRAFT comparison paragraph with formal analysis, (c) inference-time steering baseline or clear justification for its absence, (d) missing citations addressed, and (e) human annotation of a sample of masked sycophancy cases.\n","numLines":174,"startLine":1,"totalLines":174}}}
